WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Good morning, John. So, this video’s gonna be about birth control and statistics.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.580
So if you’re not super down talking about
sex or math,

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.740
or you get the wibblies thinking about me talking about either of those things,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.280
I don’t mind if you check out on this one.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:16.380
For those of you who stayed, I remember when I first took sex ed., I was told that condoms had a 2% failure rate.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.220
And there’s about 25 different ways that
you can misunderstand that statistic, so I wanna talk about that.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.000
First, what kind of failure?

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.720
‘Cause condoms do more than one thing.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.620
They reduce the chances of pregnancy, but
also of disease transmission.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.660
Or when they say “failure,” do they mean,
like, a structural failure of the condom,

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.699
which may or may not result in pregnancy or disease transmission.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:39.840
Well, in this case, what my sex ed. teacher
was talking about (and what we’re talking about in this video) is unwanted pregnancy.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.440
So my teacher was saying when using a condom perfectly and properly for vaginal sex, there is a 2% chance of pregnancy occurring.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.660
But, like, per what? I thought, at the time, that it was per sex. That makes a certain amount of sense.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.880
So like every time you do it, you have a 2%
chance of pregnancy, and that would be pretty terrible.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.860
Like that would be just 10 times better than just having unprotected sex.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:06.140
But whenever you read about unintended pregnancy rates with birth control, that’s per year of use, not per individual intercourse.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.020
So over a year of perfect, proper condom use, there is a 2% chance that a couple will get pregnant.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.460
Not a couple. Just the one person, but, you know.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:23.580
This actually, for me, is a little less good
because one sexually active person might have way—like ten times—more sex than another sexually active person.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.980
And the person who has more sex will be more likely to get pregnant using only condoms.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:34.100
But that’s how it gets measured because,
for the most part, these statistics aren’t meant to help individuals make individual
decisions.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.290
They’re meant to inform public health decisions by policymakers.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.590
But they get used by individuals because they’re the only stats we have.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:47.180
Per active intercouse, the odds of pregnancy decrease about 250 times if you’re using a condom.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.320
That’s a lot better than just ten times.

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:57.760
And that is a statistic that, as far as I
can tell, has never been taught in a sex ed. course because I had to calculate it myself using somewhat incomplete raw data.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.340
Now, in addition to misunderstanding what the 2% statistic meant, I also misunderstood how statistics work.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:10.800
And I thought that meant that if there was
a 2% chance of unintended pregnancy, that if you did it a hundred times, there would be two pregnancies.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.360
That’s not how probabilities work. You have a 2% chance every year.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.900
So you have a 2% chance the first year, a
2% chance the tenth year, a 2% chance the hundredth year, though not actually.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.720
On the hundredth year, seems unlikely.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.920
Every year, it’s a new roll of the dice.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.650
But statistics does allow us to measure probabilities for multiple rolls of the dice.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:33.220
So, if you go for two years, you multiply
.98 by .98 and get your odds of not getting pregnant.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:39.320
If you use condoms perfectly every time for ten years, your odds become about 20% that you will get pregnant.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.720
And over twenty years, that becomes one in three.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:48.040
Now, the final and vital way that this statistic is misunderstood is that perfect use is very different from typical use.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:55.960
And when we measure typical use, we see that condoms result in pregnancy about 20% of years in which people only use condoms.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.120
That is a very different number from 2%.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.380
Typical use is how the average person uses birth control.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:07.320
And you may not be an average person—nobody really is—but if there’s a way to mess up, people often find that way.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:17.060
Which is why new forms of birth control, like implants or IUDs if they work for that particular person, have had a really substantial impact on unwanted pregnancies in the world and in America.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:23.620
These technologies’ typical use is the exact same as their perfect use because they’re just always doing the thing they’re supposed to do.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.480
And they’re also even more effective than
perfect use of condoms.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.860
Though neither of them, to be clear, protect against diseases, obviously.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:34.540
John, I made this video because I was really confused when I was given these statistics.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:41.520
I wish that I had had more than the basketball coach begrudgingly parroting some politicized curriculum at me.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.440
Hopefully, the existence of the internet and continuing work of scientists to understand this stuff

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:54.000
will allow people to get better
information and make better decisions even as people continue to fight to hide this stuff.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.700
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

